<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="practice-definite-integrals">

    <title>Definite Integrals</title>

    <exercise xml:id="practice-definite-integrals-1">
        <title>Approximating and Evaluating a Definite Integral</title>

        <introduction>
            <p>
                Let <m>f(x)=e^x+\cos(x)</m>.
            </p>
        </introduction>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Approximate <m>\int_0^\pi f(x)\,dx</m> using a midpoint
                    Riemann sum with <m>n=4</m> equal subintervals.
                    Give your answer in exact form.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    The width of each subinterval is
                    <m>\Delta x=\pi/4</m>, and the midpoints are
                </p>

                <me>
                    \frac{\pi}{8},\quad
                    \frac{3\pi}{8},\quad
                    \frac{5\pi}{8},\quad
                    \frac{7\pi}{8}.
                </me>

                <p>
                    Therefore, the midpoint sum is
                </p>

                <me>
                    M_4
                    =
                    \frac{\pi}{4}
                    \left[
                    f\left(\frac{\pi}{8}\right)
                    +f\left(\frac{3\pi}{8}\right)
                    +f\left(\frac{5\pi}{8}\right)
                    +f\left(\frac{7\pi}{8}\right)
                    \right].
                </me>

                <p>
                    The cosine terms cancel, so
                </p>

                <me>
                    M_4
                    =
                    \frac{\pi}{4}
                    \left(
                    e^{\pi/8}
                    +e^{3\pi/8}
                    +e^{5\pi/8}
                    +e^{7\pi/8}
                    \right).
                </me>
            </solution>
        </task>

        <task>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Apply Part II of the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus
                    to evaluate <m>\int_0^\pi f(x)\,dx</m> exactly.
                </p>
            </statement>

            <solution>
                <p>
                    An antiderivative of <m>f(x)</m> is
                    <m>F(x)=e^x+\sin(x)</m>. Therefore,
                </p>

                <me>
                    \int_0^\pi
                    \left(e^x+\cos(x)\right)\,dx
                    =
                    \left[e^x+\sin(x)\right]_0^\pi
                    =
                    e^\pi-1.
                </me>
            </solution>
        </task>

    </exercise>


    <exercise xml:id="practice-definite-integrals-2">
        <title>Evaluating an Integral Geometrically</title>

        <statement>
            <p>
                Evaluate <m>\int_{-2}^{2}\sqrt{4-x^2}\,dx</m>.
                Explain how the geometry of the graph allows you to
                determine the value of the integral.
            </p>
        </statement>

        <solution>
            <p>
                The graph
                <m>y=\sqrt{4-x^2}</m>
                is the upper semicircle of the circle
                <m>x^2+y^2=4</m>, which has radius <m>2</m>.
            </p>

            <p>
                Therefore, the integral is the area of a semicircle
                of radius <m>2</m>:
            </p>

            <me>
                \int_{-2}^{2}\sqrt{4-x^2}\,dx
                =
                \frac{1}{2}\pi(2)^2
                =
                2\pi.
            </me>
        </solution>
    </exercise>

</section>